<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Corcyre (department)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1797–1799</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Corcyre was a French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> created in 1797 from the former Venetian Ionian Islands after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-campo-formio">Treaty of Campo Formio</a>, named after the ancient Greek name of Corfu. Centred on Corfu, it was one of three departments, with Ithaque and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mer-egee">Mer-Égée</a>, into which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> organised the islands and their mainland outposts. It ended when a Russian-Ottoman expedition captured Corfu in 1799.</p></div>
